464 0 obj
<<
/Title (Why the C Flag is ON: The math overflowed the top of the 3-bit limit. The final column produced a leftover 1 \(Carry-Out\) that had nowhere to go.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 441 0 R
/Next 471 0 R
>>
endobj
465 0 obj
<<
/Title (How the CPU detects V: The hardware calculates XOR between the Carry-In \(the bit brought into the last column\) and the Carry-Out \(the bit pushed out of the last column\). Because those two bits do not match, the V flag turns on.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 441 0 R
/Prev 471 0 R
>>
endobj
466 0 obj
<<
/Title (When you ask the computer to check if \(A > B\) or \(A < B\), the CPU  calculates \(A - B\). It does this by using the hardware trick we discussed earlier: flipping the bits of  B adding 1,  and then adding the result to A.  If result is positive that means A is greater than B.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 442 0 R
/Next 472 0 R
>>
endobj
